# Requirements

## Hypotheses

### H1: Zero input leaves the body at rest

WHEN all five inputs c0 to c4 are held at 0 for the whole trial, THE system SHALL end within 0.05 m of the start in x.

```prediction
# relies on: none
[[prediction]]
id = "P1"
measurement = "x"
range = [-0.05, 0.05]
label = "inside"
[prediction.when]
a0 = 0.0
a1 = 0.0
a2 = 0.0
a3 = 0.0
a4 = 0.0
```

Result: supported. T1 x = 2.5e-5 m. Constant c0=1 and constant c1=1 also left the body at rest (x about 1e-5 to 3e-5). The picture shows a small body with one thin limb, no motion.

### H2: Quadrature sinusoids on c0 and c1 move the body

WHEN c0 = sin(2 pi t / 1 s) and c1 = cos(2 pi t / 1 s) at amplitude 1 and c2 to c4 are 0, THE system SHALL end at least 0.05 m from the start in x.

```prediction
# relies on: none
[[prediction]]
id = "P1"
measurement = "x"
range = [0.05, 1.0]
label = "new-combination"
[prediction.when]
a0 = 1.0
a1 = 1.0
a2 = 0.0
a3 = 0.0
a4 = 0.0
period = 1.0
```

Result (E2): rejected. c0 sin plus c1 cos gave x = 3.9e-5 m, no motion. c2 sin plus c3 cos (T5) gave x = 0.042 m, y = 0.089 m, heading wobbling about +-10 deg, final -4.2 deg. c4 sin alone (T6) gave x = 0.002 m, heading drifting to 5.8 deg. So c2 and c3 drive motion and c0, c1 do nothing in this form.

### H3: Slower or reversed c2/c3 quadrature gives more forward travel

WHEN c2 = sin(2 pi t / 2 s) and c3 = cos(2 pi t / 2 s) at amplitude 1 and c0, c1, c4 are 0, THE system SHALL end with x between 0.1 and 0.6 m.

```prediction
